Let S be a semitopological semigroup, i.e., a semigroup endowed with a Hausdorff topology such that for every s ∈ S the maps t ↦ s·t and t ↦ t·s are continuous. Let (E, Q) be a separated locally convex topological vector space whose topology is generated by a saturated family Q of seminorms. An action of S on a subset K of E is a map S × K → K, (s, x) ↦ s·x, satisfying (st)·x = s·(t·x). The action is called:
• separately continuous if for each s ∈ S the map x ↦ s·x is continuous and for each x ∈ K the map s ↦ s·x is continuous;
• weakly separately continuous if the analogous continuity holds with respect to the weak topology of E;
• Q-nonexpansive if q(s·x − s·y) ≤ q(x − y) for every s ∈ S, every q ∈ Q, and all x, y ∈ K;
• weakly equicontinuous if {x ↦ s·x : s ∈ S} is equicontinuous on K with respect to the weak topology;
• weakly quasi-equicontinuous if the closure of {x ↦ s·x : s ∈ S} in the product space K^K endowed with the weak pointwise convergence topology consists of weakly continuous maps.

Define the following fixed point properties of S:
(fppw)  Whenever S acts on a weakly compact convex subset K of a separated locally convex space (E, Q) by a weakly separately continuous, Q-nonexpansive action, K contains a common fixed point for S.
(fppwq) The same conclusion holds whenever the action is moreover weakly quasi-equicontinuous.
(fppwe) The same conclusion holds whenever the action is moreover weakly equicontinuous.
(fppQ)  Whenever S acts on a (norm/Q-)compact convex subset K of a separated locally convex space (E, Q) by a separately continuous, Q-nonexpansive action, K contains a common fixed point for S.

It is known that the implications (fppw) ⇒ (fppwq) ⇒ (fppwe) ⇒ (fppQ) hold for every semitopological semigroup S.

Question: Can any of these three implications be reversed? That is, does there exist a semitopological semigroup S for which one of (fppQ), (fppwe), (fppwq) holds while the next stronger property in the chain fails, or are some (or all) of these four properties in fact equivalent?

